**assistant**:
> `xtable`’s `digits` argument controls *numeric formatting only*. It does **not** round or format character columns, and it also won’t work if the length or structure of `digits` doesn’t match what `xtable` expects.
> 
> From your output, a few things stand out:
> 
> 1. **Your `digits` vector has length 11**  
>    ```r
>    digits = c(0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2)  # length 11
>    ```
>    But the printed table has:
> 
>    - 1 (implicit) row-number column
>    - 10 data columns:
> 
>      ```text
>      persist, 0%, 5%, 50%, 95%, 100%, miniso, frac.miniso, maxiso, frac.maxiso
>      ```
> 
>    So there are **1 + 10 = 11 columns** in the LaTeX table (including the row-number column). That means `digits` must have **length 11**, which you do have. So the length itself is fine.
> 
> 2. **Some of your columns are character, not numeric**  
>    In the table body:
> 
>    ```text
>    ... 12.7445650357702 & NER & 17.2305409394077 & NOR & 42.521328226079
>    ```
> 
>    - `miniso` looks like `"NER"`, `"SDN"`, etc. → **character**
>    - `maxiso` looks like `"NOR"` → **character**
>    - The rest (e.g., `-14.565260342583`, `17.2305409394077`, `42.521328226079`) are numeric.
> 
>    `xtable`’s `digits` only applies to numeric columns. Character columns are printed as-is, unaffected by `digits`.
> 
> 3. **Why are the numeric columns still showing many decimals?**  
>    By default, `print.xtable()` *should* respect the `digits` stored in the xtable object. But `print(xtable(tbl, digits=...))` does two things in one:
> 
>    - Creates the `xtable` object
>    - Immediately prints it, possibly with default `digits` again
> 
>    In some setups, or depending on other arguments, this can be confusing. To be explicit and avoid surprises, split the steps and/or give `digits` to `print.xtable` directly:
> 
>    ```r
>    library(xtable)
> 
>    xt <- xtable(tbl)       # or xtable(tbl, digits = digits)
>    print(xt, digits = c(0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2))
>    ```
> 
>    Or:
> 
>    ```r
>    xt <- xtable(tbl, digits = c(0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2))
>    print(xt)    # should now respect the stored digits
>    ```
> 
> 4. **How digits are mapped to columns**
> 
>    For a data frame with `k` columns, `digits` for `print.xtable` must be length `k + 1`:
> 
>    - `digits[1]`: used for the row names/index column
>    - `digits[2]`: first data column (`persist`)
>    - `digits[3]`: second data column (`0%`)
>    - …
>    - `digits[11]`: last data column (`frac.maxiso`)
> 
>    So for your table:
> 
>    ```r
>    digits <- c(
>      0,  # row names
>      2,  # persist
>      2,  # 0%
>      2,  # 5%
>      2,  # 50%
>      2,  # 95%
>      2,  # 100%
>      0,  # miniso (character: ignored, but must have a slot)
>      2,  # frac.miniso
>      0,  # maxiso (character: ignored)
>      2   # frac.maxiso
>    )
>    ```
> 
>    This is structurally correct; just keep in mind character columns won’t show any rounding.
> 
> 5. **Quick minimal reproducible pattern**
> 
>    Try this pattern with your actual `tbl`:
> 
>    ```r
>    library(xtable)
> 
>    digits <- c(0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2)
>    xt <- xtable(tbl)
>    print(xt, digits = digits)
>    ```
> 
>    If you still see full-precision decimals, check:
> 
>    ```r
>    str(tbl)
>    ```
> 
>    to ensure the columns you expect to format are truly numeric (not factors or character).
> 
> If you paste the output of `str(tbl)` I can tell you exactly which entries in `digits` are actually having an effect and suggest the precise vector you need.
