**assistant**:
> There are two separate issues here:
> 
> 1. **Your `digits` vector is fine and is being accepted.**
> 2. **`xtable` will not round values that are already character strings.**
> 
> What’s likely happening is that the numeric-looking columns in `tbl` are actually **character** (or factor) columns, so `xtable` is just printing their strings verbatim and ignoring `digits`.
> 
> ### 1. Verify the column types
> 
> Run:
> 
> ```r
> str(tbl)
> sapply(tbl, class)
> ```
> 
> If you see `"character"` or `"factor"` for columns like `0%`, `5%`, `50%`, etc., then `digits` cannot affect them. `xtable` only formats columns of class `"numeric"` / `"integer"`.
> 
> If they’re *not* numeric, convert them before calling `xtable`:
> 
> ```r
> tbl2 <- tbl
> # Example: convert all non-character, non-factor columns to numeric
> num_cols <- sapply(tbl2, function(x) !is.character(x) & !is.factor(x))
> tbl2[num_cols] <- lapply(tbl2[num_cols], as.numeric)
> 
> xt <- xtable(tbl2)
> print(xt, digits = c(0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2))
> ```
> 
> Or explicitly by name:
> 
> ```r
> tbl2 <- transform(
>   tbl,
>   persist      = as.numeric(persist),
>   `0%`         = as.numeric(`0%`),
>   `5%`         = as.numeric(`5%`),
>   `50%`        = as.numeric(`50%`),
>   `95%`        = as.numeric(`95%`),
>   `100%`       = as.numeric(`100%`),
>   frac.miniso  = as.numeric(frac.miniso),
>   frac.maxiso  = as.numeric(frac.maxiso)
> )
> xt <- xtable(tbl2)
> print(xt, digits = c(0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2, 0, 2))
> ```
> 
> After that, you should see numbers like `-14.57` instead of `-14.565260342583`.
> 
> ### 2. Confirm that `print` does see your `digits`
> 
> To be sure `print.xtable` is getting your vector, try:
> 
> ```r
> getOption("digits")           # this is a *different* global setting
> xt <- xtable(tbl2)
> print(xt, digits = digits, include.rownames = TRUE)
> ```
> 
> If you accidentally have a *matrix* `digits` (e.g., via `as.matrix(digits)`), or a named vector that doesn’t match column count, `xtable` will silently fall back to column-wise defaults. Your plain numeric vector of length 11 is structurally correct, so the only remaining explanation is column classes.
